WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.170
In rare cases, a truly terrible performance
can actually improve a movie, transforming

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:09.219
the mediocrity on display into something so
bad that it turns full circle and becomes

00:00:09.219 --> 00:00:12.129
great in its own right, or at least enjoyable.

00:00:12.129 --> 00:00:15.890
Join us as we take a look at the dreadful
roles that might never win an Oscar, but that

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:17.820
actually managed to make a film better.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.780
Everyone's favorite flower shop customer typifies
the kind of bad acting that people enjoy more

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:23.560
than any other.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:24.420
"Hi, doggie."

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.280
"You're my favorite customer."

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.060
"Thanks a lot.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:26.840
Bye."

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.880
After writing, directing, and starring in
2003's truly inexplicable The Room, a film

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.530
often described as the Citizen Kane of bad
movies, Tommy Wiseau became an industry legend

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:38.360
in ways that even he couldn't predict.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:43.780
Fifteen years after Wiseau made his big screen
debut, the actor's bizarre accent, exaggerated

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.860
delivery, and sheer lack of acting ability
have transformed The Room into a cult classic

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.160
that continues to be quoted by midnight movie
fans worldwide.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.620
In the hands of anyone else, a script like
this would just be bad, but Wiseau's baffling

00:00:55.629 --> 00:01:00.269
performance somehow elevates these words into
a bizarrely hilarious curio that will forever

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:02.220
hold a place in Hollywood's hall of fame.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.660
"You're lying, I never hit you.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.540
You are tearing me apart, Lisa!"

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.560
In the end, Wiseau's performance was so bad
that it wound up inspiring an entire award-winning

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:13.060
movie.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.100
In 2017, James Franco starred as Wiseau in
The Disaster Artist, a movie based on the

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.880
making of The Room, affecting his own inexplicable
accent and picking up a Golden Globe for his

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:23.420
troubles.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.220
That's one of the few times that anyone's
been given an award for being good at bad

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:27.720
acting.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.080
Sometimes, a movie deserves every bit of its
bad reputation, and 1995's allegedly erotic

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.480
thriller Showgirls is definitely not great.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.440
According to director Paul Verhoeven, however,
that was always the intent.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:45.240
Elizabeth Berkley, who starred as Nomi Malone,
deliberately pushed her trashiness to previously

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.670
unseen extremes precisely because he told
her to, in an effort to deliver exactly the

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.640
kind of performance that he felt that the
script from Joe Eszterhaus required.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:53.940
"Well it ain't right.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.640
You got too much TALENT for it to be right!"

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:57.500
"Get outta here!"

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.700
While critics trashed the movie when it hit
theaters, audiences have since learned to

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.180
embrace the ludicrous dialogue and campy acting
of Showgirls, holding a particularly special

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.740
place in their hearts for Berkley and the
way she played Nomi.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.410
From her cringeworthy attempts at dancing
to that infamous scene in the swimming pool,

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.360
Berkley's performance has become one of the
all-time greats in cult cinema.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.560
It's just a shame that her attempts to turn
people on ultimately turned casting directors

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:22.060
off.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.200
In an effort to distance herself from the
good girl she'd played on Saved by the Bell,

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.360
Berkley wound up tanking her acting career
at what could've been its height.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.000
After returning to the spotlight with his
iconic role in Quentin Tarantino's Pulp Fiction

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.560
in 1994, John Travolta could've done just
about anything.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.640
Unfortunately, what he decided to do with
his new-found glory and millions of his own

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.080
dollars was produce a sci-fi epic that became
one of the most notorious flops in cinema

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:46.580
history.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.840
Based on a novel written by L. Ron.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.030
Hubbard, the founder of Scientology, Battlefield
Earth became a legendary failure for both

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:54.400
audiences and fans.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.400
Virtually every aspect of the film was panned,
with critics condemning everything from the

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.040
confused direction to the ludicrous plot holes,
but particular attention was given to Travolta's

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:03.880
role in the film.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.970
"With endless options for renewal!

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:07.540
With endless options for renewal!

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:09.320
With endless options for renewal!

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.900
HAHAHA!"

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:17.300
The long-time Scientologist played Terl, a
member of the Psychlos, Battlefield Earth's

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:21.970
ten foot-tall world-conquering aliens who
kept "man-animals" as slaves to turn a profit

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.180
for a space corporation.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:27.720
Short of twiddling an imaginary moustache,
Travolta's pantomime performance went so far

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.900
over the top that it became the only thing
in a movie that was otherwise mind-bogglingly

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.090
monotonous that was actually fun to watch.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:40.420
"While you were still learning how to SPELL
your name, I was being trained to conquer

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:41.100
galaxies!"

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.340
Despite its now legendary critical mauling,
Travolta still maintains that Battlefield

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:50.020
Earth is actually an underrated classic that
doesn't deserve its bad reputation.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.920
He's even claimed in interviews that if he
had to do it all over again, he'd still take

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.220
the role, blaming the media for its negative
image.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:00.220
Sounds like he really did want those endless
options for renewal after all.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:05.720
In a misguided attempt to recapture the campy
magic of the '60s Batman TV show, director

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:10.110
Joel Schumacher and writer Akiva Goldsman
set the superhero genre back decades with

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.460
their second collaboration starring the Dark
Knight.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:17.060
From its gaudy production values to those
unforgettable bat-nipples, Batman &amp; Robin

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:22.320
has regularly topped lists compiling the worst
movies of all time since its release in 1997.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.200
Both Schumacher and star George Clooney have
even apologized for their roles in bringing

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:30.651
the film to life, regretting how Batman &amp; Robin
temporarily brought the franchise to an end

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.520
before it was rebooted eight years later.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.440
However, anyone who's seen the film knows
that no apology is necessary.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.080
It might have some pretty significant flaws,
but it's a movie that knows exactly what it

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.941
wants to be, and also brought us the campy
delight that is Arnold Schwarzenegger in the

00:04:43.941 --> 00:04:45.180
role of Mr. Freeze.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:46.460
"Show some mercy!"

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.100
"MERCY?!

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.880
I'm afraid that my condition has left me cold."

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.100
In a huge departure from the more serious
action roles that he was known for, like Terminator

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:02.280
and Predator, Schwarzenegger's take on the
ice cold villain took action movie puns to

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:02.960
the next level.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.060
"What killed the dinosaurs?

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:07.680
The ice age!"

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:12.880
Freeze takes great delight in turning every
line into a gag, and, in hindsight, it's hard

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.080
to imagine anyone else but Arnie managing
to get through any of it without either breaking

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.380
into laughter or bailing on the film entirely.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:25.460
1987's big-screen adaptation of Masters of
the Universe was Inspired by the beloved '80s

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:30.650
cartoon and toy line, mixing Mattel's story
of He-Man and Eternia with elements from unrelated

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:33.880
comics by Jack Kirby, the co-creator of the
Marvel Universe.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.920
Given He-Man's popularity and how much people
have turned out to love Kirby's creations

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.640
in their massive cinematic universe, that
probably seems like a good idea for a blockbuster

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:43.140
franchise.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:44.340
It was not.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:51.240
"I, Skeletor, am Master of the Universe."

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.020
Poor reviews and lackluster box office earnings
put an end to hopes for a sequel, and even

00:05:56.030 --> 00:06:00.850
contributed to the imminent closure of B-movie
icon Cannon Films, but it seems that most

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:01.920
people missed the point.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:07.180
Sure, He-Man's ongoing war against Skeletor
is clunky and poorly made, but Masters Of

00:06:07.181 --> 00:06:11.610
The Universe also deserves to be celebrated
as a campy classic in its own right, mostly

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.120
due to Dolph Lundgren's performance in the
lead role.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:16.540
"I'll never kneel to you!"

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:17.980
"Yes you will!

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:19.840
Yes you will!"

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:24.700
Wielding a sword and little else, Lundgren
imbued He-Man with a goofy charm that resonated

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.650
with an entire generation of children who
were overjoyed to see their hero on the big

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:29.620
screen at last.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:33.740
Using any measure known to man, it's clear
that Lundgren's performance will never be

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:38.660
considered as "good" in any objective interpretation
of the word, but his portrayal of the character

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:43.360
on screen has made what could've been a forgotten
toy tie-in into a fascinating cult classic.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:49.699
Like Robin Hardy's original 1973 take on The
Wicker Man, the 2006 remake revolves around

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.800
the search for a missing girl on a remote
island.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:56.560
However, unlike the first Wicker Man film,
the newer version starring Nicolas Cage omits

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.370
the horror in favor of unintentionally hilarious
comedy, spawning hundreds of snarky reviews

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:04.360
from critics and countless more memes from
the internet at large.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:05.660
"How'd it get burned?

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:06.580
How'd it get burned?

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:08.160
How'd it get burned?

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:08.900
How'd it get burned?"

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:13.800
Viewed purely as a remake of the horror classic,
the 2006 version of The Wicker Man is definitely

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:14.660
not a good film.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:19.720
After all, the original remains a masterclass
in suspense and paranoia, and this new imitation

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:20.900
simply doesn't hold up.

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:25.010
However, if you're willing to put aside any
notions of horror and embrace Cage's bizarre

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:29.380
performance for what it is, then the new Wicker
Man is arguably one of the best comedies ever

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:29.880
made.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:30.700
"Oh, what is that?!

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:31.200
What is it?!

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:33.820
OH, NO NOT THE BEES!

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:35.360
NOT THE BEES!

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:37.400
AHHHH!

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:38.980
AH THEY'RE IN MY EYES!"

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:43.080
It's hard to tell whether Cage played the
part for laughs or was genuinely sincere in

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.000
the role, but either way, his (over-)acting
here is truly a marvel to behold.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:51.320
With an animal-like intensity, Cage blazes
across the screen, screaming about bees and

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.480
punching women while wearing a bear costume.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:57.540
There's nothing else out there quite like
it, and if the film's box office failure is

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:01.560
anything to go by, then there will probably
never be anything like this again.

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:07.080
It's rather ironic that a movie about a futuristic
cop that acts as judge, jury, and executioner

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:11.020
has become a guilty pleasure for many, but
that's exactly what's happened in the years

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:14.540
since Sylvester Stallone brought Judge Dredd
to the big screen in 1995.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.200
"Drop your weapons!

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:22.940
These blocks are under arrest!"

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.080
The film was originally considered to be a
box office flop domestically, and since it

00:08:27.081 --> 00:08:32.140
lacked the satirical tone and stern, stone-faced
scowl of the original British comic book character,

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:36.420
it didn't resonate with casual moviegoers
or die-hard fans of 2000 AD.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:40.900
For that, fans would have to wait another
17 years, but interest in the property has

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:44.360
ensured that Stallone's Judge Dredd will never
be forgotten completely.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:48.160
One particular sticking point for fans of
the comic was the fact that Stallone took

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.460
off the helmet and revealed Dredd's face in
the movie.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:54.340
This is something that the character would
have never done in the source material, and

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:56.480
still hasn't in over 40 years of comics.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.420
Then again, if Stallone hadn't wanted to get
his mug on camera, we would have never received

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:03.240
the gift of his hilariously goofy facial expressions.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:04.810
"For Judge Joseph Dredd."

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:05.900
"It's a lie!

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.180
The evidence has been falsified!

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:09.340
It's impossible!

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:10.860
I never broke the law!

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:12.240
I am the law!"

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.360
The sheer absurdity peaks at the end of the
film, during an argument shared between Dredd

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.890
and his evil clone, Rico, escalating to the
point where both forget how to pronounce basic

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:22.890
words in English.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:24.070
"You killed innocent people!"

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:25.220
"The means to an end!"

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:26.220
"You started a massacre!"

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:27.220
"I was the revolution!"

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:28.360
"You betrayed the law!"

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:29.800
"LAWWWWW!"

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:34.560
The gibberish that both actors scream at each
other has since become immortalized online

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.360
thanks to an endless stream of parodies that
ensure Judge Dredd will hold a special place

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.480
in the hearts of cinephiles everywhere.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.970
Plus, we got a much more accurate adaptation
simply titled Dredd in 2012 that did a far

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:50.279
better job of capturing what the character
is really all about, making Stallone's cinematic

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:52.700
failure a lot easier for fans to take.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:57.680
In the same tradition as The Beatles' A Hard
Day's Night in 1964 and The Monkees' cult

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.500
film Head in 1968, The Spice Girls brought
their own unique brand of girl power to cinemas

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:07.470
everywhere in 1997, capitalizing on both their
record-breaking success and the world's brief

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:09.180
fascination with "Cool Britannia."

00:10:09.340 --> 00:10:13.900
Predictably, Spice World broke box office
records, and, even more predictably, the critics

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:15.560
loathed it for the most part.

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:19.170
However, even the most hardened journalist
must admit that there's something infectiously

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:20.170
fun about the film.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:23.830
Sure, there's not much of a plot to speak
of and, yes, none of the girls ever made the

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:27.760
headlines for their acting ability, but thanks
to the bubbly charisma of the stars and a

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:32.581
roster of wild cameos that includes Elvis
Costello and Meat Loaf in a minor role, Spice

00:10:32.581 --> 00:10:37.010
World continues to spice up the lives of fans
everywhere two decades on.

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:40.930
Spice World even got a re-release in U.K.
cinemas to celebrate its 20th anniversary

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:45.060
in 2017, suggesting that the Spice Girls still
retain their retro charm.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:49.260
Since Spice World was first released, the
girls have gone their separate ways and reunited

00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:52.180
more than once, but none of that matters while
watching the film.

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:57.300
Spice World is a bright and breezy time-capsule
of a unique period in pop music history, one

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:01.510
that wouldn't work without the brash positivity
of the girls themselves, each of whom elevate

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:06.000
the plot's silly trappings into something
undeniably silly yet ultimately engrossing.

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:11.260
Every now and again, a movie comes along that
defies the odds and fails in every conceivable

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:11.760
way.

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.940
More than any other film on this list, Troll
2 is that movie.

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:19.240
From the totally implausible plot to the fact
that not even a single troll appears in the

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:24.940
film, this 1990 no-budget disaster is a catastrophe
in every sense of the word, redefining what

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:26.620
the word "bad" truly means.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:27.640
"Michael?"

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:28.800
"Yeah?"

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:31.780
"Who are the Goblins?"

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:36.520
The story itself follows a family who try
and stop vegetarian goblins from turning them

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:38.620
into plants, but that doesn't really matter.

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:43.180
What does matter is the atrocious performances
on display, which helped the film develop

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:47.640
a cult following that led to numerous screenings
at midnight movie festivals and a documentary

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:52.680
called Best Worst Movie in 2009 which chronicled
the many problems experienced on set.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:57.120
While it's difficult to pinpoint the single
worst performance in Troll 2, the one that's

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.029
gained the most notoriety over the years comes
courtesy of Darren Ewing, whose character

00:12:01.029 --> 00:12:05.270
reacts in horror at the sight of a woman being
devoured by goblins and delivers a line that

00:12:05.270 --> 00:12:07.400
would become a viral sensation years later.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:08.740
"They're eating her!

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:11.680
And then they're going to eat me!

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.980
OH MY GOOOOO--"

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:22.400
Sure, it's a bad movie, maybe the worst movie,
but there's no way to watch that clip without

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:26.310
having a fun time watching it, and there are
plenty of Hollywood blockbusters that can't

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:27.200
claim that.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:29.420
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:32.680
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
stuff are coming soon.

